\section*{Uniform duality for convex-body covering entropy}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 329; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that the proposed primal--polar entropy comparison fails for any choice of universal constants, even if one covers by translates of a cube.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 329; 2026-09-24), partitions compress convex-hull columns uniformly across coordinates. Finite-field zero estimates supply many separated rows whose dual images remain compressible; choosing the matrix parameters makes the primal entropy arbitrarily larger than the proposed polar comparison.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 329; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 329; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: the proposed primal--polar entropy comparison fails for any choice of universal constants, even if one covers by translates of a cube.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{MetricEntropyDuality.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.MetricEntropyDuality.exists\_entropy\_duality\_counterexample\_with\_covers}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

